==German==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|de|Dolmetsch||archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|tolmetsche}} (13th c.), ultimately from {{der|de|trk-pro|*tilmaç}} (compare {{cog|tr|dilmaç}}). Probably borrowed through a {{der|de|sla|-}} language; compare  {{cog|sla-pro|*tъlmačь||interpreter}}, whence {{cog|ru|sc=Cyrl|толма́ч}}, {{cog|pl|tłumacz}}. Alternatively through {{der|de|hu|tolmács}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈdɔlmɛtʃər/|[ˈdɔl.mɛ.t͡ʃɐ]|[-ˌmɛ-]}}
* {{audio|de|De-Dolmetscher.ogg|audio}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|m||Dolmetscher|f=Dolmetscherin}}

# [[interpreter]] {{gloss|one who translates in real time; male or of unspecified sex}}
# {{lb|de|archaic}} [[translator]] (male or of unspecified sex)
#: {{syn|de|Übersetzer}}

====Usage notes====
* While it remains common in general parlance to use {{m|de||Übersetzer}} as an umbrella term, it is now unusual to refer to a translator of books or other completed texts as a {{m|de||Dolmetscher}}.

